<p>For the browser automation, <code>playwright-core</code> drives <strong>a properly installed Edge or Chrome</strong> directly. The reason it does not use the downloaded cache browser is what real corporate machines are like — on some company PCs a security agent blocks the executables under the <code>ms-playwright</code> cache, and there the cache browser hangs without bringing up even a blank page. The system browser works fine under the same conditions. The lookup order is the <code>SG_CHROMIUM_PATH</code> environment variable → the system Edge/Chrome → the cache browser.</p>

<h3>Installing it</h3>
<p>The tool paths are relative to the project root, so unpack it at <strong>the project root you open Claude Code in</strong>.</p>
<pre><code>my-project/
├── .claude/
│   ├── skills/dews-sourcegraph/SKILL.md      <span class="c"># the skill body</span>
│   └── tools/sourcegraph-search.mjs …        <span class="c"># the runner + package.json</span>
└── SourceGraph.md                            <span class="c"># credentials (you write this)</span></code></pre>
<p>Merge the zip's <code>.claude</code> folder into your project root, then copy <code>SourceGraph.md.example</code> to <code>SourceGraph.md</code> and fill in three lines.</p>
<pre><code>URL:https://&lt;sourcegraph-host&gt;/
ID:(account ID)
PW:(password)</code></pre>
<p>To keep it elsewhere, point the <code>SG_CRED_FILE</code> environment variable at that path. Then install the dependencies and restart Claude Code, and the skill turns up in the skill list.</p>
<pre><code>cd .claude/tools
npm install</code></pre>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Two things you must not skip</span>
    <strong>① Never commit <code>SourceGraph.md</code> or <code>.claude/tmp/</code></strong> — the account and the session cookie leak into the repository. Add the two lines to <code>.gitignore</code>.<br>
    <strong>② When a login fails, do not retry</strong> — consecutive password failures lock the account for a while. Read the server message the tool prints (<code>User or password was incorrect</code> / <code>locked out</code>), fix the cause, and try again exactly once.
</div>

<h3>How to use it</h3>
<p>Tell Claude "find ○○ on SourceGraph", or call it directly with <code>/dews-sourcegraph</code>. You can also run it straight from the terminal.</p>
<pre><code>node --no-warnings .claude/tools/sourcegraph-search.mjs --query "myFunction file:\.java$ count:30" --max 20</code></pre>
<p>Queries use Sourcegraph's standard syntax as it is.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Filter</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>(bare term)</td><td><code>myFunction</code></td><td>search the code contents</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>repo:</code></td><td><code>repo:my-service</code></td><td>limit to a repository-name pattern</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>file:</code></td><td><code>file:\.java$</code></td><td>file-path regex</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>type:</code></td><td><code>type:path</code></td><td>match filenames only, ignore contents</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>count:</code></td><td><code>count:all</code></td><td>server-side result cap</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>-</code> exclude</td><td><code>-file:bin/</code></td><td>drop build-artifact noise</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Add <code>--pattern regexp</code> when you need a regular expression. Zero results usually means the vocabulary is off, so it is faster to strip the filters, throw one wide query, correct your wording from what comes back, and narrow again.</p>

<h3>Why it digs through the local code first</h3>
<p><strong>Local first</strong> is nailed into the skill document as a rule, because a company-wide search does not always give the better answer. Conventions differ from team to team even for the same feature, and a pattern that clashes with the existing code in your own repository comes back at review. So the trigger is narrowed to three cases — when no precedent of the same kind exists locally, when you have only a derived copy and need to confirm which repository the original lives in, and when you need to see the internals of a shared library or the places that call it.</p>
<p>The citation format is settled too. Evidence obtained by search is left as <code>[SG {repo}/{file}:{line} → pattern to reproduce]</code> and treated at the same grade as the <code>file:line</code> evidence of a local precedent. If the whole original is needed, the skill first checks whether that repository is present locally and, if it is not, asks the user whether to clone it. Search and excerpt is where this skill's boundary lies.</p>

